#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575672} :activity/id #uuid "2919bf66-4175-573b-8a63-c19cdbbc6283" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049421 :topic/id #uuid "f3745544-3239-55ba-94eb-3deb858524c5" :topic/math-academy-id 1941} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "140b554f-cda0-547b-9903-50ee55410862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,-1,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574982} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae0a243-805e-5331-aeaf-9970f13c6bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,-\\frac{1}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575140} {:answer/id #uuid "909b3046-2008-57b1-b5df-b71974be8bd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575198} {:answer/id #uuid "b219bccd-abfe-5f1e-9522-0fceb1758979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575267} {:answer/id #uuid "bad99448-6cc8-5829-8b73-b4d8c01e4ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,-2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575282}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186574982} :answer-field/id #uuid "1589433d-cfd2-5a34-998a-3aeb17064001" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6269cc62-3112-546a-8502-b50893c81d78" :question/math-academy-id "q-106587" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨-2,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,-2,0⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨-2,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,-2,0⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,-2,0)-f(-2,0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨0,-2,0⟩-⟨-2,0,1⟩) \\\\ -4-1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,-2,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨0,-2y,2z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨0,-2{c}_{2},2{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =⟨0,-2{c}_{2},2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =4{c}_{2}-2{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-(-2)}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{(-2)}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ \\frac{x+2}{2} & =-\\frac{y}{2}=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=2t-2,\\,y=-2t,\\,z=1-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2t-2,\\,{c}_{2}=-2t,\\,{c}_{3}=1-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $4{c}_{2}-2{c}_{3}=-5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-2t)-2(1-t) & =-5 \\\\ -8t-2+2t & =-5 \\\\ -6t & =-3 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2(\\frac{1}{2})-2=-1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-2(\\frac{1}{2})=-1, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(-1,-1,\\frac{1}{2}).$"} :step/id #uuid "2d167846-fff1-5ca3-8a4b-f2b5c4b3f716"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00c83848-790a-57b8-8602-083229057a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4})$ and $(1,-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574952} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc38ae2-b8b1-599a-9f26-312cb01336da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(1,-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575019} {:answer/id #uuid "61eccc28-259a-5f54-8bb4-fe001dda7b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(1,-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575126} {:answer/id #uuid "d011184c-1551-5de6-9144-2a3823d1eda3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0,1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575318} {:answer/id #uuid "fb36eb9a-d973-59d4-9f42-30b5098b0e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0,1)$  and $(\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575399}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575019} :answer-field/id #uuid "29f40bfe-790d-5cc9-8bed-56827e2c9958" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "aab55964-3387-5606-94f7-4fdb235f672a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106586" :question/problem "Find the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{z}^{3}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,-1,0⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{z}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,-1,0⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,-1,0)-f(0,0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,-1,0⟩-⟨0,0,1⟩) \\\\ 1-(-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 2 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨0,2y,-3{z}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨0,2{c}_{2},-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 2 & =⟨0,2{c}_{2},-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 2 & =-2{c}_{2}+3{c}_{3}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-0}{(-1)}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ x & =-y=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t\\,\\,y=-t,\\,z=1-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=-t,\\,{c}_{3}=1-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $-2{c}_{2}+3{c}_{3}^{2}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(-t)+3(1-t)^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2t+3{t}^{2}-6t+3 & =2 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-4t+1 & =0 \\\\ (3t-1)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{3},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $(\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-1, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(1,-1,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "638938db-de39-5dd0-b8e6-446d4857c5e5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575642}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9171ed9d-70aa-51df-aeed-e5ec02c4a3a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575201} {:answer/id #uuid "a34be0b0-f3d4-5dba-9880-eceaf7d7e3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575235} {:answer/id #uuid "b7377674-4129-5146-89ba-a65a50896132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575274} {:answer/id #uuid "d2584164-f33e-5a05-87c7-b1c5385ed371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575321} {:answer/id #uuid "dc33850d-9b75-50ba-b0d0-a512650bf018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575341}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575201} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e0aed6d-6aa4-591d-a07c-58e38ed2d1dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "551372ab-8536-5d27-a0f1-42bcde72d2ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-106067" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨-1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨2,-2⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨-1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨2,-2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,-2)-f(-1,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,-2⟩-⟨-1,1⟩) \\\\ ((-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+1)-({1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,-3⟩ \\\\ 9-3 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,-3⟩ \\\\ 6 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,2y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},2{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-3⟩ \\\\ 6 & =⟨2{c}_{1},2{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-3⟩ \\\\ 6 & =6{c}_{1}-6{c}_{2} \\\\ 1 & ={c}_{1}-{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{-2-1} & =\\frac{x-(-1)}{2-(-1)} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{(-3)} & =\\frac{x+1}{3} \\\\ y-1 & =-(x+1) \\\\ y & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =-{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation ${c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-(-{c}_{1}) & =1 \\\\ 2{c}_{1} & =1 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$"} :step/id #uuid "93a1c49d-34ab-59cc-ad60-a38a9ccf8093" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575673}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575673 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575542 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "099e07ff-2aa2-51ce-8ec5-e580916dce57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574964} {:answer/id #uuid "4ecbea3d-3768-526f-8f06-5921414062ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,-8)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575082} {:answer/id #uuid "7344b7b9-f641-5fed-b0e8-3e2ad93004d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575161} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7b1906-bd98-5dd9-9960-0ccd94df2fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{1}{2},-3)$ and $(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575261} {:answer/id #uuid "f5167de5-1b72-575f-8c14-8ac0426c389b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,-\\frac{16}{3})$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575381}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575381} :answer-field/id #uuid "656518d1-a3d5-5c0a-b159-52a12f937780" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4d8fdbb6-cc34-5cc1-be22-82ba675a440f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106091" :question/problem "Determine the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=y-{x}^{3}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨-2,-8⟩$ and $b=⟨1,0⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=y-{x}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨-2,-8⟩$ and $b=⟨1,0⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0)-f(-2,-8) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,0⟩-⟨-2,-8⟩) \\\\ (0-{1}^{3})-(-8-(-2)^{3}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,8⟩ \\\\ -1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-3{x}^{2},1⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-3{c}_{1}^{2},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,8⟩ \\\\ -1 & =⟨-3{c}_{1}^{2},1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,8⟩ \\\\ -1 & =-9{c}_{1}^{2}+8 \\\\ 9{c}_{1}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {c}_{1}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-(-8)}{0-(-8)} & =\\frac{x-(-2)}{1-(-2)} \\\\ \\frac{y+8}{8} & =\\frac{x+2}{3} \\\\ y+8 & =\\frac{8(x+2)}{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{8(x-1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{8({c}_{1}-1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For ${c}_{1}=-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=\\frac{8(-1-1)}{3}=-\\frac{16}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the first point are $(-1,-\\frac{16}{3}).$\n\n- For ${c}_{1}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=\\frac{8(1-1)}{3}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the second point are $(1,0).$\n\nFinally we can conclude that the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem are $(-1,-\\frac{16}{3})$ and $(1,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "93fa2235-c9de-5716-b816-04a781c4344a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575657}}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Mean-Value Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574941}]}
